# Shell scripts are LF, everywhere, always.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout rewrites them to CRLF on the way
# out of git. A shell script with CRLF line endings has `\r` on the end of its
# shebang, so the kernel looks for an interpreter called `/bin/sh\r` and the
# container dies with `no such file or directory` — an error that names the
# script, not the line endings, and reads as a missing file.
#
# It cost time here: `apps/grow/docker-entrypoint.sh` was written LF, survived
# one docker build, then came back CRLF from a `git stash pop` and broke the
# next one. The repository always held LF, so Coolify's Linux build was never
# at risk — but a local build must behave the same way as the real one, or it
# is not a test of anything.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles and compose files are read by the same Linux tooling.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
